1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
To fly beyond the speed of sound.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A dangerous scientific journey.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The building of an airplane like no other,

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
flown by pilots determined to succeed or die trying.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, breaking the sound barrier on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:31,001
On October 14, 1947, at a remote base in the California desert,

7
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:38,380
24-year-old Air Force Captain Charles Chuck Yeager prepared to

8
00:00:38,381 --> 00:00:42,740
board an experimental airplane called the XS-1 and try to do

9
00:00:42,741 --> 00:00:46,380
what none had done before, fly beyond the speed of sound.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,900
You get assigned to a program, you fly.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,400
And really, you don't look at the results down the road.

12
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:54,900
You're going to fly the airplanes,

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,940
and we're shooting for the next high step.

14
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:00,940
For decades, the sound barrier loomed

15
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,760
as an impenetrable wall against manned flight,

16
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,660
pummeling airplanes with violent shock waves when they approached

17
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,760
it and causing some, like the P-38 Lightning,

18
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:15,760
to lose control in high-speed dives with disastrous results.

19
00:01:15,761 --> 00:01:18,660
The sound barrier became the stuff of epic myth.

20
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:20,660
It was not merely a knowledge barrier,

21
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,160
but might, in fact, be an actual physical barrier

22
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:24,760
that we could not overcome.

23
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:29,100
On that day in 47, Jaeger had a physical handicap.

24
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,500
He was nursing two broken ribs.

25
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:33,920
But as the sun rose slowly over the desert,

26
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:35,920
he told his backup pilot, Bob Hoover,

27
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:38,420
that he was ready to meet the challenge.

28
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:43,120
I asked him if he felt good enough to fly the airplane.

29
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:44,660
There would have been no way in the world

30
00:01:44,661 --> 00:01:47,160
to keep Chuck out of that cockpit.

31
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:50,300
We're working test pilots, and duty enters into it.

32
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:52,260
It doesn't make any difference.

33
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,400
You got a job to do, you do it.

34
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:58,300
Yeager would be assisted by a B-29 bomber crew

35
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:02,720
piloted by Robert Cardenas, which would carry the XS-1

36
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:06,420
to high altitude and launch it into the sky.

37
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,060
Then Yeager and his aircraft would hurtle toward the sound

38
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:12,720
barrier and discover what lay beyond.

39
00:02:12,721 --> 00:02:14,720
This is going to be it.

40
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,460
If it works, we've done it.

41
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:22,900
If it doesn't, well, there's going to be hell to pay.

42
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,600
The sound barrier.

43
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:28,740
The name alone suggested a force both strange and sinister.

44
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,700
Yet by definition, it is simply the speed at which sound waves

45
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:36,180
travel, about 760 miles an hour at a sea level temperature of

46
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:40,480
59 degrees, and more slowly at higher altitudes, where the air

47
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:45,020
the air gets colder. The waves of water produced by the

48
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:49,980
bow of a ship are similar to those in air which flow past a moving plane.

49
00:02:49,981 --> 00:02:55,480
At slow speeds, that air is smoothly displaced by a plane's forward motion.

50
00:02:55,481 --> 00:03:00,820
But when traveling closer to the speed of sound, in what is called the transonic zone,

51
00:03:00,821 --> 00:03:05,920
the waves become more compressed, and those compressions force some of the airflow beyond

52
00:03:05,921 --> 00:03:09,620
speed of sound. The result is a mix of erratic

53
00:03:09,621 --> 00:03:13,720
airflow and powerful shock waves which buffet the plane while

54
00:03:13,721 --> 00:03:16,560
impeding its lift and controllability.

55
00:03:16,561 --> 00:03:20,160
You can think of waves in water as being equivalent to waves

56
00:03:20,161 --> 00:03:23,600
in air, and those are very much like the shock waves that would

57
00:03:23,601 --> 00:03:26,840
form over an aircraft. As the shock waves move back and

58
00:03:26,841 --> 00:03:29,920
forth very rapidly, you have this separation area that

59
00:03:29,921 --> 00:03:32,080
hammers on the part of the airplane.

60
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:34,780
If that happens to be a control surface, you can lose control of

61
00:03:34,781 --> 00:03:37,720
aircraft. The speed of sound can be roughly

62
00:03:37,721 --> 00:03:40,780
calculated by the time it takes to hear a noise from a measurable

63
00:03:40,781 --> 00:03:44,880
distance. In 1877, Austrian physicist

64
00:03:44,881 --> 00:03:48,020
Ernst Mach devised a formula by which the speed of a moving

65
00:03:48,021 --> 00:03:52,400
object is divided by the speed of sound to calculate its Mach

66
00:03:52,401 --> 00:03:55,460
number. An object moving at the speed

67
00:03:55,461 --> 00:04:00,900
of sound has reached Mach 1. In the decades following the

68
00:04:00,901 --> 00:04:05,860
first flight in 1903, scientists theorized that as an aircraft

69
00:04:05,861 --> 00:04:09,460
approached Mach 1, the resistance from air pressure would soar

70
00:04:09,461 --> 00:04:13,300
toward infinity and tear the airplane apart.

71
00:04:13,301 --> 00:04:16,340
But in an age when propeller planes flew with speeds far below

72
00:04:16,341 --> 00:04:19,460
the sound barrier, these doomsday predictions caused

73
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:24,340
little concern. And throughout the 1930s, as the

74
00:04:24,341 --> 00:04:27,460
development of rocket and jet propulsion systems began to

75
00:04:27,461 --> 00:04:30,340
suggest the potential for supersonic flight, the American

76
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:32,380
the American aviation establishment

77
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:35,480
lagged well behind its European rivals.

78
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:38,280
Our technical grasp of aeronautics was deficient.

79
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,620
We had really lost competitiveness

80
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:41,880
with the European nations.

81
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:48,500
World War II changed the picture.

82
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:51,360
Speed became essential to air combat.

83
00:04:51,460 --> 00:04:54,560
Scrambling to catch up, the US government poured money

84
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:56,060
into the research and development

85
00:04:56,061 --> 00:05:00,360
of faster fighter planes, like the B-51 Mustang.

86
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:03,260
But while executing sharp high-speed dives,

87
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:08,000
pilots sometimes experienced a frightening loss of control.

88
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,600
We just start shaking.

89
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:13,240
I mean, it just, it let you know that you're in an area

90
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:14,320
you shouldn't be in.

91
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:18,720
I used to hear from the fighter boys, you know,

92
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:22,020
old Joe's wing blew off and nobody was shooting at him.

93
00:05:22,021 --> 00:05:27,300
They would come back and tell the engineers the plane froze

94
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,400
up.

95
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:31,600
The controls in a dive were unresponsive.

96
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:34,660
Meanwhile, Germany launched its supersonic V2

97
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:37,640
rocket in 1942.

98
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:42,600
Two years later, the Luftwaffe were flying jets into combat.

99
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,640
Had the war lasted longer, some believed

100
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:47,680
that Germany's superior aviation technology might have changed

101
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:49,540
its outcome.

102
00:05:49,541 --> 00:05:52,480
As we went through the rubble of Nazi Germany,

103
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:56,440
we discovered how really narrow our technological victory over

104
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,140
the Third Reich had been.

105
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,960
We had missed in large measure the significance

106
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:02,460
of high speed flights.

107
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:06,820
And it quickly became apparent that technology industry was

108
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:09,400
going to have to address this issue.

109
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:12,600
Propelled by a sense of urgency in the wake of the war,

110
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,400
the US vowed to build an airplane

111
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:18,040
to see if the sound barrier could be safely breached.

112
00:06:18,041 --> 00:06:20,740
But the magnitude of that ambition demanded cooperation

113
00:06:20,741 --> 00:06:24,180
from two powerful agencies, which didn't always see eye

114
00:06:24,181 --> 00:06:27,980
to eye, the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics,

115
00:06:27,981 --> 00:06:32,980
precursor to today's NASA, and the US Army Air Forces.

116
00:06:32,981 --> 00:06:35,600
Though both groups agreed on the importance of the mission,

117
00:06:35,601 --> 00:06:38,700
they sharply disagreed on how to get there.

118
00:06:38,701 --> 00:06:41,900
There were two distinct points of view about how to go about

119
00:06:41,901 --> 00:06:46,540
the supersonic project. The NACA traditionally felt

120
00:06:46,541 --> 00:06:50,240
the best way of pursuing research was to do so incrementally.

121
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:54,580
The Air Force appreciated this type of research,

122
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:58,180
but they wanted as few delays as possible.

123
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:02,080
In March of 1945, Bell aircraft owner Larry Bell

124
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:04,900
agreed to construct a plane for the government, which would be

125
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
safe and controllable up to 0.80 Mach, a speed at the lower

126
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:12,840
end of the transonic zone, and with enough power in reserve

127
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:15,100
to fly beyond Mach 1.

128
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:19,580
The airplane would be called the XS-1, X for experimental, S

129
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:23,880
for sonic, and one to denote the first of its kind.

130
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:27,140
With little evidence to suggest what kind of plane to build,

131
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:29,180
Bell took a common sense approach,

132
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:31,080
designing the nose of the fuselage

133
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:34,080
in the shape of a .50 caliber bullet.

134
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:36,020
They knew that the .50 caliber bullet,

135
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,120
as it decelerated back through the speed of sound,

136
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:41,600
had stable deceleration characteristics

137
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:43,860
and had relatively low drag.

138
00:07:43,861 --> 00:07:47,000
Bell also decided to use a rocket engine rather than a jet

139
00:07:47,001 --> 00:07:51,000
system to propel the airplane. Unlike a rocket with its

140
00:07:51,001 --> 00:07:54,700
self-contained fuel supply, turbojet engines draw air from

141
00:07:54,701 --> 00:07:57,900
the atmosphere for thrust. And those in development were not

142
00:07:57,901 --> 00:08:00,100
capable of maintaining sufficient thrust at high

143
00:08:00,101 --> 00:08:04,220
altitudes, where the XS-1 would be flown to take advantage of

144
00:08:04,221 --> 00:08:07,360
lower air resistance. Only rocket power would

145
00:08:07,361 --> 00:08:11,660
produce the type of power necessary to allow this plane

146
00:08:11,661 --> 00:08:16,560
to fully explore the transonic zone and perhaps go supersonic.

147
00:08:16,561 --> 00:08:20,360
Anticipating violent shockwaves near the sound barrier, Bell

148
00:08:20,361 --> 00:08:24,600
built the XS-1 to withstand 18 Gs, twice the strength of

149
00:08:24,601 --> 00:08:28,040
conventional fighter planes. Yet its wings, fortified by a

150
00:08:28,041 --> 00:08:32,040
skin of milled aluminum, were unusually thin to help maintain

151
00:08:32,041 --> 00:08:37,520
lift in the transonic zone. To further offset control

152
00:08:37,521 --> 00:08:40,960
problems at transonic speeds, the tail of the XS-1 was

153
00:08:40,961 --> 00:08:44,960
than usual on the vertical fin. Another innovation called the

154
00:08:44,961 --> 00:08:48,960
adjustable horizontal stabilizer allowed the pilot to manually

155
00:08:48,961 --> 00:08:52,960
adjust the pitch of the entire tail from inside the plane,

156
00:08:52,961 --> 00:08:56,960
creating, in effect, a movable tail.

157
00:08:56,961 --> 00:09:00,960
But if protecting the XS-1 from the elements was a priority,

158
00:09:00,961 --> 00:09:04,960
protecting its pilot was not. The cockpit had no ejection seat,

159
00:09:04,961 --> 00:09:07,960
and bailing out from the side entrance meant risking decapitation

160
00:09:07,961 --> 00:09:11,460
decapitation from the wings, which ran parallel to the door.

161
00:09:11,461 --> 00:09:17,600
There was no practical way to exit the airplane, absolutely

162
00:09:17,601 --> 00:09:21,600
none whatsoever. As Chuck Yeager later on said,

163
00:09:21,601 --> 00:09:24,560
you know, I wore the parachute, but basically I wore it just to

164
00:09:24,561 --> 00:09:26,960
sit on because there's no way I could have gotten out of that

165
00:09:26,961 --> 00:09:30,500
airplane. Compounding the danger,

166
00:09:30,501 --> 00:09:34,200
contemporary wind tunnels, the usual method employed to predict

167
00:09:34,201 --> 00:09:37,620
an airplane's controllability at varying speeds, were not yet

168
00:09:37,621 --> 00:09:40,760
accurate readings near the speed of sound.

169
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:44,660
Wind tunnels were surprisingly deficient.

170
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:47,260
We really didn't quite know what was happening there.

171
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,260
And so we'd use the sky basically as a laboratory.

172
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:53,300
With so few scientific signposts,

173
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:56,900
flying the XS-1 would require not just technical savvy,

174
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,500
but raw courage.

175
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:01,280
Hardened and tested by war, such pilots

176
00:10:01,380 --> 00:10:03,720
were readily at hand to accept the challenge.

177
00:10:03,721 --> 00:10:08,520
challenge. The founder of Bell Aircraft,

178
00:10:08,521 --> 00:10:11,960
Larry Bell, once developed a makeshift bomber for Poncho

179
00:10:11,961 --> 00:10:14,220
Villa. Breaking the Sound Barrier

180
00:10:14,221 --> 00:10:22,020
will return on Modern Marvels. We now return to Breaking the

181
00:10:22,021 --> 00:10:28,020
Sound Barrier on Modern Marvels. World War II was a conflict

182
00:10:28,021 --> 00:10:31,280
defined by air power. Dogfights over Europe were

183
00:10:31,281 --> 00:10:35,280
battles that pushed pilots and planes to the limit.

184
00:10:35,281 --> 00:10:38,280
During dogfights, you'd go into high-speed dives or as fast as

185
00:10:38,281 --> 00:10:41,280
you could get the airplane going straight down from, you know,

186
00:10:41,281 --> 00:10:44,280
36,000, 37,000 feet. And when this happened, a shockwave

187
00:10:44,281 --> 00:10:47,280
formed on the thickest part of the wing. And behind the shock

188
00:10:47,281 --> 00:10:50,280
wave, you had turbulent air. And it was just a nuisance if

189
00:10:50,281 --> 00:10:55,280
you're trying to track some guy to shoot him down.

190
00:10:55,281 --> 00:10:58,280
After the war ended, Chuck Yeager and Bob Hoover were among

191
00:10:58,281 --> 00:11:02,280
were among 125 Army Air Force pilots stationed at Ohio's

192
00:11:02,281 --> 00:11:06,280
Wright Field. There they improved their skills

193
00:11:06,281 --> 00:11:09,280
flying American planes and captured German aircraft, which

194
00:11:09,281 --> 00:11:12,280
were brought there to test their flight characteristics.

195
00:11:12,281 --> 00:11:15,280
We were flying so many different types of airplanes

196
00:11:15,281 --> 00:11:20,280
in a given month that it would be incomprehensible to an Air

197
00:11:20,281 --> 00:11:25,280
Force pilot nowadays. Yeager and Hoover also attended

198
00:11:25,281 --> 00:11:27,280
Wright Field's flight test school.

199
00:11:27,281 --> 00:11:30,320
Army Air Force Colonel Albert Boyd, who ran the school,

200
00:11:30,321 --> 00:11:33,720
wished to expand the military's role in flight testing and

201
00:11:33,721 --> 00:11:37,820
development. He saw the need for test

202
00:11:37,821 --> 00:11:41,220
pilots to become not just proficient stick and rudder men,

203
00:11:41,221 --> 00:11:44,940
but proficient technically, to become true engineering test

204
00:11:44,941 --> 00:11:48,640
pilots. The Army Air Forces

205
00:11:48,641 --> 00:11:52,140
controlled the budget for the XS-1 project as well, but flight

206
00:11:52,141 --> 00:11:54,740
research was traditionally done by the National Advisory

207
00:11:54,741 --> 00:11:58,980
Committee for Aeronautics, along with private contractors.

208
00:11:58,981 --> 00:12:01,480
So when the first XS-1 was rolled out for its maiden

209
00:12:01,481 --> 00:12:06,480
flights at Florida's Pine Castle Field in January of 1946, Bell

210
00:12:06,481 --> 00:12:09,780
Aircraft directed the early tests with the assistance of the

211
00:12:09,781 --> 00:12:14,100
NACA, and it was a civilian pilot from Bell, Jack Woolloms,

212
00:12:14,101 --> 00:12:19,440
who flew the airplane. The NACA had the charter for

213
00:12:19,441 --> 00:12:23,700
flight research, the test pilots there at Wright Field, really

214
00:12:23,701 --> 00:12:29,240
kind of fell into a secondary status. With the XS-1 rocket

215
00:12:29,241 --> 00:12:32,780
engine still in development, Jack Woolloms piloted 10 glide

216
00:12:32,781 --> 00:12:36,880
flights at Pine Castle. To do that, the XS-1 was lowered

217
00:12:36,881 --> 00:12:40,380
into a loading pit, where it was hoisted into the bomb bay of a

218
00:12:40,381 --> 00:12:45,120
B-29 by means of a standard bomb shackle.

219
00:12:45,121 --> 00:12:49,100
The B-29 was modified for the job by making cuts in its fuselage

220
00:12:49,101 --> 00:12:52,360
to accommodate the smaller plane, which was painted saffron

221
00:12:52,361 --> 00:12:58,360
to make it more visible. Then the B-29 flew to an altitude

222
00:12:58,361 --> 00:13:03,600
of 22,000 feet and dropped the XS-1 into the sky.

223
00:13:03,601 --> 00:13:07,320
The XS-1 performed well during the glide tests, but Florida's

224
00:13:07,321 --> 00:13:10,360
rainy weather, along with some minor landing mishaps, caused

225
00:13:10,361 --> 00:13:13,360
several delays and canceled flights.

226
00:13:13,361 --> 00:13:17,360
So in the summer of 1946, the government moved the XS-1

227
00:13:17,361 --> 00:13:21,360
program from Pine Castle to Murak Army Airfield in Southern

228
00:13:21,361 --> 00:13:25,920
California, a desert base whose main natural asset, Rogers Dry

229
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:31,360
Lake, constituted an ideal landing field.

230
00:13:31,361 --> 00:13:34,000
But Jack Woolhams never made it to Muroc.

231
00:13:34,001 --> 00:13:38,120
In August of 1946, while testing his P-39 Cobra 1 race

232
00:13:38,121 --> 00:13:41,320
plane for an air show in Cleveland, he was killed in a

233
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:45,720
crash. He was replaced in the XS-1 by another Bell pilot named

234
00:13:45,721 --> 00:13:51,360
Chalmers Slick Goodlin. Chalmers Slick Goodlin was

235
00:13:51,361 --> 00:13:54,560
He was an experienced World War II pilot.

236
00:13:54,561 --> 00:13:59,900
He was young, 23, and he was known to be a good stick and

237
00:13:59,901 --> 00:14:05,100
rudder man. By December of 1946,

238
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:08,240
the XS-1 rocket engine was ready for Slick Goodland's first

239
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:11,240
powered flight. The engine, which weighed only

240
00:14:11,241 --> 00:14:15,880
208 pounds, was composed of four steel cylinders, each capable of

241
00:14:15,881 --> 00:14:20,920
generating 1,500 pounds of static thrust.

242
00:14:20,921 --> 00:14:24,920
oxygen and water-diluted ethyl alcohol. Before each flight,

243
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:27,920
mechanics on the base would pump those propellants into separate

244
00:14:27,921 --> 00:14:30,920
spherical tanks located behind the cockpit.

245
00:14:30,921 --> 00:14:33,920
Pressurized nitrogen gas force-fed the propellants into the

246
00:14:33,921 --> 00:14:36,920
rocket chambers, where they could be ignited, either

247
00:14:36,921 --> 00:14:40,920
separately or all at once. Nitrogen was also used to

248
00:14:40,921 --> 00:14:43,920
pressurize the cockpit and to control the wing flaps and

249
00:14:43,921 --> 00:14:47,920
landing gear. The XS-1 was also used to

250
00:14:47,921 --> 00:14:51,920
The X-S-1 was capable of a ground takeoff, which would

251
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:55,920
allow the NACA to conduct a full range of flight tests.

252
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:59,160
But the heavy and voluminous fuel supply system nearly doubled

253
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:02,760
the weight of the plane, making an air launch the more

254
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:05,980
practical option for reaching the sound barrier.

255
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:11,180
This effectively cut the flying time of the X-1 at full power

256
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:14,980
to 2 and 1 half minutes. With only 2 and 1 half minutes,

257
00:15:14,981 --> 00:15:18,980
that settled the issue. Flights at Muroc

258
00:15:18,981 --> 00:15:22,480
proceeded cautiously. The NACA had loaded the plane

259
00:15:22,481 --> 00:15:26,220
with 500 pounds of instrumentation and could measure data

260
00:15:26,221 --> 00:15:31,220
from 300 pressure points on the wing, making the XS-1 the most

261
00:15:31,221 --> 00:15:34,340
thoroughly instrumented plane of its time.

262
00:15:34,341 --> 00:15:37,340
We had instrumentation there that would show the stability

263
00:15:37,341 --> 00:15:39,740
and control of the airplane. We had instrumentation of what

264
00:15:39,741 --> 00:15:42,740
the pilot was doing. It was heavily loaded for a reason,

265
00:15:42,741 --> 00:15:47,780
reason, because we looked at all of this data in order to predict what we did the next time.

266
00:15:47,781 --> 00:15:52,180
There's no point doing the flights just to do them, to say that you punched through the sound

267
00:15:52,181 --> 00:15:56,820
barrier, if you don't have a clear trail of evidence that tells you how it happened.

268
00:15:56,821 --> 00:16:00,207
The NACA's record keeping was meticulous.

269
00:16:00,208 --> 00:16:04,420
After six months and 22 flights, Slick Goodland had

270
00:16:04,421 --> 00:16:09,495
flown the XS-1 to .82 Mach, the lower region of the transonic zone.

271
00:16:09,496 --> 00:16:11,540
But when the NACA predicted

272
00:16:11,541 --> 00:16:14,380
that at least 50 more flights would be necessary to challenge

273
00:16:14,381 --> 00:16:17,820
the sound barrier, the military became concerned about the pace

274
00:16:17,821 --> 00:16:19,520
of the program.

275
00:16:19,521 --> 00:16:21,140
They believed that breaking the barrier

276
00:16:21,141 --> 00:16:22,860
would have far-reaching consequences

277
00:16:22,861 --> 00:16:26,980
for future armed conflicts, and that whoever broke it first

278
00:16:26,981 --> 00:16:29,400
would gain the upper hand.

279
00:16:29,401 --> 00:16:31,260
There was a very strong recognition

280
00:16:31,261 --> 00:16:35,140
among the American military that when the war ended in 1945,

281
00:16:35,141 --> 00:16:39,240
a new war was about to begin, a war for technological supremacy

282
00:16:39,241 --> 00:16:41,880
in the post-World War II era.

283
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:44,280
Bell Aircraft was worried as well.

284
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:47,140
The company was being paid on a fixed contract basis,

285
00:16:47,240 --> 00:16:48,880
and time meant money.

286
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:51,980
So in the spring of 1947, they submitted their own proposal

287
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:54,280
to take over the next phase of testing,

288
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:56,400
but with a guarantee that the government would pick up

289
00:16:56,500 --> 00:17:00,300
the costs of the program no matter how long it took.

290
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:03,540
Bell needed to resolve these issues very, very quickly,

291
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:05,540
because if it didn't, its economic livelihood

292
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:07,940
was going to be at stake.

293
00:17:07,941 --> 00:17:10,600
But military budgets had been slashed after the war,

294
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:14,380
and the Bell proposal, which included a sizable flight bonus for its pilot,

295
00:17:14,600 --> 00:17:18,300
Slick Goodland, was rejected by the Army Air Forces as too expensive.

296
00:17:19,460 --> 00:17:24,860
It had been common practice since the 1930s for these civilian contractor pilots

297
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:28,640
to get bonuses for a first flight in an airplane

298
00:17:28,641 --> 00:17:32,240
or some kind of highly risky flight or series of flights.

299
00:17:32,241 --> 00:17:37,280
It became an issue because the Army Air Forces didn't have any money.

300
00:17:37,940 --> 00:17:40,940
When Bell rejected the government's counteroffer, the

301
00:17:40,941 --> 00:17:43,940
Army Air Forces suddenly had an opportunity to take over the

302
00:17:43,941 --> 00:17:48,940
XS-1 program and move it along at a faster pace.

303
00:17:48,941 --> 00:17:51,940
But the stakes were high. The success or failure of the

304
00:17:51,941 --> 00:17:55,940
XS-1 could determine the extent of the military's involvement

305
00:17:55,941 --> 00:17:59,940
in future flight research. With so much riding on the

306
00:17:59,941 --> 00:18:03,940
outcome of the program, the choice of which military pilot

307
00:18:03,941 --> 00:18:07,380
pilot would fly the XS-1 became crucial.

308
00:18:07,381 --> 00:18:10,840
That decision fell to Wright Field's Colonel Boyd.

309
00:18:10,841 --> 00:18:13,080
Boyd was faced with what he later called one

310
00:18:13,081 --> 00:18:15,280
of the most important and difficult decisions

311
00:18:15,281 --> 00:18:16,280
he had to make.

312
00:18:16,281 --> 00:18:22,200
So he has to pick an airman to fly this airplane who's

313
00:18:22,201 --> 00:18:26,420
responsible but who is not overly cautious.

314
00:18:26,421 --> 00:18:28,800
And the pick he makes is Chuck Yeager.

315
00:18:28,801 --> 00:18:31,100
And what impressed him so much about Yeager

316
00:18:31,101 --> 00:18:35,100
was the fact that he had this remarkable ability.

317
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:37,460
Hemingway called it grace under pressure.

318
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:41,460
The priorities were, as Colonel Boyd laid him down,

319
00:18:42,060 --> 00:18:44,400
said, get the airplane above Mach 1 as soon as you can,

320
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:48,140
and, you know, don't kill yourself

321
00:18:48,141 --> 00:18:49,500
and don't embarrass the Air Force.

322
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:52,000
Boyd picked Yeager's close friend,

323
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:54,820
25-year-old Bob Hoover, as his backup pilot.

324
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:57,980
He said, do you know Chuck Yeager?

325
00:18:57,981 --> 00:19:01,220
And I said, yes, sir, I certainly do.

326
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:03,660
He said, well, what do you think of him?

327
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:06,520
I said, he's the best aviator I've ever seen.

328
00:19:07,620 --> 00:19:10,980
Boyd selected a 32-year-old pilot and Caltech graduate

329
00:19:10,981 --> 00:19:13,840
named Jack Ridley as the XS-1 flight engineer.

330
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:16,940
Yeager and Ridley quickly formed a tight bond.

331
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:21,060
Ridley was a technical guy, very well educated,

332
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:24,400
and he understood technology much, much more than I did.

333
00:19:24,401 --> 00:19:33,600
and Colonel Boyd really saw that here Ridley is my brain on these deals that I really didn't

334
00:19:33,601 --> 00:19:37,164
understand, and he knew he'd keep me straight.

335
00:19:37,165 --> 00:19:40,960
In the summer of 1947, Yeager, Ridley, and Hoover

336
00:19:40,961 --> 00:19:46,552
traveled to the Bell Plant in Niagara Falls for their first close look at the XS-1.

337
00:19:46,553 --> 00:19:47,360
The airplane

338
00:19:47,361 --> 00:19:51,360
The plane was small, about 30 feet long and 10 feet high, but

339
00:19:51,361 --> 00:19:55,360
powerful. They flipped the switch, ignited

340
00:19:55,361 --> 00:19:58,360
one rocket. Christ, it made a hell of a noise,

341
00:19:58,361 --> 00:20:01,360
and some of the plaster shook off the roof. It was pretty

342
00:20:01,361 --> 00:20:04,360
impressive. Colonel Boyd dispatched 27-year-old

343
00:20:04,361 --> 00:20:08,360
B-29 pilot Roberto Cardenas and his flight engineer Edward

344
00:20:08,361 --> 00:20:13,360
Swindell, 31, to join Yeager's team at Muroc.

345
00:20:13,361 --> 00:20:17,760
with Bell project engineer Dick Frost, 29, these young men all

346
00:20:17,761 --> 00:20:22,160
under the age of 40 would determine the fate of the XS-1.

347
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:26,800
You find a group of people here who were unified and who

348
00:20:26,801 --> 00:20:29,920
worked together extraordinarily well.

349
00:20:29,921 --> 00:20:32,720
But even as the newly assembled flight team prepared to

350
00:20:32,721 --> 00:20:36,320
take over the XS-1, scientists were still debating whether the

351
00:20:36,321 --> 00:20:38,720
sound barrier could be conquered at all.

352
00:20:38,721 --> 00:20:42,320
Several months before, British test pilot Jeffrey de Havilland,

353
00:20:42,321 --> 00:20:45,560
son of the founder of England's de Havilland Aircraft Company,

354
00:20:45,561 --> 00:20:48,560
had been killed when his plane disintegrated while training to

355
00:20:48,561 --> 00:20:52,320
break the world speed record in a dive, a crash attributed to

356
00:20:52,321 --> 00:20:54,560
the effects of transonic shockwaves.

357
00:20:54,561 --> 00:20:58,560
But the members of the newly formed XS-1 team would not be

358
00:20:58,561 --> 00:21:03,080
easily dissuaded from their task. We were fearless, I

359
00:21:03,081 --> 00:21:05,200
think. That's about the best way to

360
00:21:05,201 --> 00:21:09,120
describe it. I mean, I can withstand anything.

361
00:21:09,121 --> 00:21:12,080
And that's the attitude you had during the war, when you'd see

362
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:15,680
shot down all around you. I wouldn't say that Chuck was

363
00:21:15,681 --> 00:21:20,080
fearless. It's just that he knew fear.

364
00:21:20,081 --> 00:21:24,720
He knew fear. He knew how to handle fear.

365
00:21:24,721 --> 00:21:29,440
By July of 1947, Yeager and the rest of the XS-1 team had

366
00:21:29,441 --> 00:21:32,640
moved out to the California desert. The final push toward

367
00:21:32,641 --> 00:21:36,640
the sound barrier was about to begin.

368
00:21:36,641 --> 00:21:41,040
During World War II, fighter pilot Chuck Yeager shot down

369
00:21:41,041 --> 00:21:44,880
shot down five German planes in one day.

370
00:21:44,881 --> 00:21:47,680
Breaking the Sound Barrier will return on Modern Marvels.

371
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:57,180
We now return to Breaking the Sound Barrier on Modern Marvels.

372
00:21:57,181 --> 00:22:00,400
With Cold War tensions rising after World War II,

373
00:22:00,401 --> 00:22:04,680
the US was determined to keep the XS-1 program under wraps.

374
00:22:04,681 --> 00:22:07,940
And Murok Army Airfield, a rugged outpost in a sparsely

375
00:22:07,941 --> 00:22:12,940
populated desert was the ideal location for a project shrouded in secrecy.

376
00:22:12,941 --> 00:22:17,004
Muroc at the time was harsh and primitive and isolated.

377
00:22:17,005 --> 00:22:19,220
To give an example, they would

378
00:22:19,221 --> 00:22:24,080
put down bales of hay and they would lace it with barbed wire to keep rattlesnakes out

379
00:22:24,081 --> 00:22:27,140
of buildings. And in a way it gave a certain spirit

380
00:22:27,141 --> 00:22:30,540
to the place and a desire to concentrate on the work.

381
00:22:30,541 --> 00:22:37,420
Chuck Yeager's first XS-1 flight at Muroc on August 6, 1947, an unpowered glide

382
00:22:37,421 --> 00:22:40,620
set the routine for those to follow.

383
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:44,520
After the XS-1 was shackled to the B-29 piloted by Roberto

384
00:22:44,620 --> 00:22:48,660
Cardenas, Yeager flew with the crew to an altitude of 7,000

385
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:50,360
feet.

386
00:22:50,460 --> 00:22:53,460
There, he entered the XS-1 by descending a sliding lift

387
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:57,080
into the open air and squeezing into the cockpit.

388
00:22:57,180 --> 00:23:01,280
At 25,000 feet, flight engineer Jack Ridley released the shackle,

389
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:04,920
dropping the XS-1 into the sky.

390
00:23:04,921 --> 00:23:09,920
It's very smooth and quiet, very strong airplane, 18 Gs,

391
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:11,420
positive or negative.

392
00:23:11,520 --> 00:23:15,660
And my first glide flight, I mean, this thing is really neat.

393
00:23:15,760 --> 00:23:19,560
Consequently, after three glide flights, you know,

394
00:23:19,660 --> 00:23:23,780
I had no qualms about doing anything I wanted to with the

395
00:23:23,880 --> 00:23:24,880
airplane.

396
00:23:24,980 --> 00:23:27,480
But the military mission to put the XS-1

397
00:23:27,580 --> 00:23:31,280
on a fast track put Yeager and Jack Ridley at odds with Walt

398
00:23:31,281 --> 00:23:35,020
at odds with Walt Williams, the head of the NACA flight test

399
00:23:35,021 --> 00:23:38,720
unit, who wished to proceed more cautiously.

400
00:23:38,721 --> 00:23:42,160
Walt Williams at one point told me, you're going too fast.

401
00:23:42,161 --> 00:23:44,460
You're a bunch of cowboys. You're going to kill Chuck.

402
00:23:44,461 --> 00:23:46,160
You're going to ruin the airplane.

403
00:23:46,161 --> 00:23:50,800
Williams, man, is kind of a very arrogant individual.

404
00:23:50,801 --> 00:23:53,040
And the only time I ever seen him shut up was when Jack

405
00:23:53,041 --> 00:23:54,940
Ridley opened his mouth.

406
00:23:54,941 --> 00:23:57,240
Then he listened.

407
00:23:57,241 --> 00:23:59,480
There was a solution to the conflict.

408
00:23:59,481 --> 00:24:03,980
built two XS-1 planes. The NACA was granted authority to

409
00:24:03,981 --> 00:24:07,980
fly the 6063 plane through a full range of flight tests, while

410
00:24:07,981 --> 00:24:11,980
the Army Air Forces used the 6062 plane to push toward the

411
00:24:11,981 --> 00:24:16,480
sound barrier. Though the two planes looked

412
00:24:16,481 --> 00:24:19,480
nearly identical, Jaeger's airplane had slightly thinner

413
00:24:19,481 --> 00:24:23,980
wings than the NACA airplane. Thinner wings meant better lift

414
00:24:23,981 --> 00:24:27,980
and faster speed, and Jaeger and Ridley were ready to make the

415
00:24:27,981 --> 00:24:32,580
make the most of it. We're not here to get data for

416
00:24:32,581 --> 00:24:35,220
NACA. We're to get this airplane above Mach 1.

417
00:24:35,221 --> 00:24:39,220
I think that's the way at least Ridley and I looked at it.

418
00:24:39,221 --> 00:24:42,900
Underscoring his team's control of the program, Yeager inscribed

419
00:24:42,901 --> 00:24:47,140
the nose of the 6062 plane, Glamorous Glenis, after his

420
00:24:47,141 --> 00:24:51,980
wife. Yeager had done the same in World War II and figured it

421
00:24:51,981 --> 00:24:55,040
would bring him good luck. I'm the only guy flying the

422
00:24:55,041 --> 00:25:00,040
airplane. Now I could almost say it's my airplane, but it isn't.

423
00:25:00,041 --> 00:25:03,040
And I was the only guy flying it. I said, I'll put her name on it.

424
00:25:03,041 --> 00:25:07,040
Yeager's first powered flight of the XS-1 on August 27th was

425
00:25:07,041 --> 00:25:12,040
both skillful and daring. After piloting the plane to .82

426
00:25:12,041 --> 00:25:16,040
Mach on just one rocket chamber and leveling off at about 40,000

427
00:25:16,041 --> 00:25:20,040
feet, the goal of the flight plan, he went a step beyond by

428
00:25:20,041 --> 00:25:24,040
diving the airplane to 2,700 feet above the base, initiating a

429
00:25:24,041 --> 00:25:27,540
a climb, then firing all four chambers.

430
00:25:27,541 --> 00:25:29,280
And man, it really went straight up,

431
00:25:29,281 --> 00:25:30,540
and I got up to 85.

432
00:25:30,541 --> 00:25:33,540
Well, the airplane flew good, and I didn't have any qualms

433
00:25:33,541 --> 00:25:35,580
about doing it.

434
00:25:35,581 --> 00:25:39,080
By speeding to .85 Mach in his first powered flight,

435
00:25:39,081 --> 00:25:43,100
Yeager had flown the XS-1 faster than ever before

436
00:25:43,101 --> 00:25:47,600
and sent a message that the sound barrier was in his sights.

437
00:25:47,601 --> 00:25:51,100
Chuck was pretty meticulous about what he did.

438
00:25:51,101 --> 00:25:53,340
It looked like he was taking chances,

439
00:25:53,341 --> 00:25:55,540
chances, but it really wasn't.

440
00:25:55,541 --> 00:25:58,440
He'd thought things pretty well.

441
00:25:58,441 --> 00:26:01,840
But the XS-1 was still dangerous at any speed.

442
00:26:01,841 --> 00:26:04,920
Scraping the low-slung plane on the ground during takeoff

443
00:26:04,921 --> 00:26:07,760
could easily spark an explosion.

444
00:26:07,761 --> 00:26:11,160
Once in the air, Yeager had to carefully monitor the regulators,

445
00:26:11,161 --> 00:26:14,720
which controlled pressure levels in the rocket chambers.

446
00:26:14,721 --> 00:26:17,840
The margin between what was needed for maximum thrust

447
00:26:17,841 --> 00:26:21,300
and what could rupture the chambers was extremely narrow,

448
00:26:21,301 --> 00:26:24,800
just 5 pounds of pressure per square inch.

449
00:26:24,900 --> 00:26:26,740
If you let those pressures get away from you,

450
00:26:26,840 --> 00:26:29,900
you blow up.

451
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:33,240
I mean, that took a lot of your attention.

452
00:26:33,340 --> 00:26:36,040
Everything was very close to the red line.

453
00:26:36,140 --> 00:26:39,160
Everything we did was very close to the red line

454
00:26:39,260 --> 00:26:41,560
in order to get the thrust out of the engine.

455
00:26:41,660 --> 00:26:44,160
Nonetheless, Yeager pushed the XS-1

456
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:46,400
briskly toward Mach 1.

457
00:26:46,500 --> 00:26:49,600
When he reached 0.88 Mach on his second fueled flight,

458
00:26:49,601 --> 00:26:52,560
a speed well past the realm of wind tunnel tests,

459
00:26:52,640 --> 00:26:55,500
the plane began to be buffeted by shockwaves.

460
00:26:55,580 --> 00:26:57,340
But since it was still controllable,

461
00:26:57,400 --> 00:26:59,040
Yeager pressed forward.

462
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:02,540
By his sixth flight, he was flying at .92 Mach

463
00:27:02,620 --> 00:27:06,020
and racing past Bob Hoover's fleet FP-80 chase plane

464
00:27:06,080 --> 00:27:08,020
as if it were standing still.

465
00:27:08,080 --> 00:27:10,280
And then he'd go by me, and I was sitting there

466
00:27:10,360 --> 00:27:13,520
at 8 tenths Mach number, and he was going by faster

467
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:15,920
and faster each time.

468
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,260
But on October 10th, Yeager's eighth flight,

469
00:27:19,261 --> 00:27:23,600
He pushed the XS-1 to an indicated speed of .94 Mach,

470
00:27:23,700 --> 00:27:26,300
and suddenly lost control of the airplane.

471
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:28,700
And I pulled back on the control, nothing happened.

472
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:30,100
The airplane went the way it was headed,

473
00:27:30,200 --> 00:27:34,300
and the control just flopped back and forth.

474
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:36,820
Yeager finally regained control after the airplane

475
00:27:36,920 --> 00:27:39,820
slowed down and landed it safely.

476
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:41,920
And I came down and told Ridley, I said, man,

477
00:27:42,020 --> 00:27:43,460
we got a problem.

478
00:27:43,560 --> 00:27:45,860
The mood on the ground was glum.

479
00:27:45,861 --> 00:27:50,660
data showed that when the XS-1 reached 0.94 Mach, a shockwave

480
00:27:50,661 --> 00:27:54,100
began to slam directly against the hinge point of the elevator

481
00:27:54,101 --> 00:27:58,100
on the tail, freezing its mobility and thus removing

482
00:27:58,101 --> 00:28:01,700
Jaeger's ability to control the pitch of the plane.

483
00:28:01,701 --> 00:28:04,660
Since aerodynamicists believed that the impact of hitting the

484
00:28:04,661 --> 00:28:08,100
sound barrier would force the nose of a plane up or down,

485
00:28:08,101 --> 00:28:12,660
pitch control was essential. Without it, the XS-1 was too

486
00:28:12,661 --> 00:28:16,660
was too dangerous to fly. Colonel Boyd thought the project

487
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:20,660
might be doomed. Colonel Boyd came out and said,

